  "notes": [
    "Baselines are strong: the model already knows CCPM, so most assertions pass without the skill. The discriminating assertion was 'exactly one project buffer' - the lab-trials baseline used two project buffers instead of one project buffer plus a feeding buffer for the secondary deliverable.",
    "The skill's main observed value-add is consistency rather than raw correctness: a fixed schedule.csv schema, a validation gate before presenting results, deterministic tie-breaks, and integer-day arithmetic. The website-launch baseline produced fractional days (an 11.5-day buffer and a day-34.5 completion promise), which is awkward to communicate and harder to feed into downstream tooling.",
    "Cost: with-skill runs used roughly 40-60% more tokens and about 60% more wall time, spent reading the skill and running the validator loop.",
    "Assertions that passed in every configuration (PNG validity, precedence, no double-booking) do not discriminate; harder test networks would make them earn their keep."
  ]
